      "source": [
        "## 5. Why ML beats a fixed rule here\n",
        "\n",
        "*An if-statement can't search the full combination of features and thresholds needed to maximize precision — it can only encode whatever weights and cutoffs a person guesses in advance. The starter baseline does exactly this: it hand-picks weights (0.40 visibility, 0.30 freshness, 0.25 position, 0.05 depth) and six fixed thresholds (like days_since_last_update >= 180, ctr < 0.5) with no way to know if those are the right numbers. The result: Precision@50 = 0.240, barely better than chance. A decision tree, by contrast, tests many feature/threshold combinations at each split and picks whichever actually improves results — instead of a human guessing them — and a random forest (an ensemble of many such trees) pushes Precision@50 to 0.740, roughly 3x the fixed rule's performance on the same data.*"
      ],
      "id": "ON_8nAVaZnyO"
